  "caveats": [
    "HostGate is a release primitive; Runtime does not execute callback bodies or propagate their exceptions yet.",
    "Only signal() may run on a callback thread. Complete observers, cached progress and retirement still require one owner.",
    "Finish callback threads before Runtime teardown. Teardown release is cancellation, not proof a callback body ran.",
    "Gate creation/recording require open recording and idle queues; gates are not resettable or dynamically replaced on replay.",
    "Linux uses GPU semaphore polling and cached host words, not a future syncobj execbuffer wait. No CPU poll loop is added.",
    "Linux gate coherence, scheduling/preemption, page reuse and cancellation have CPU/build checks only; GPU execution remains unvalidated.",
    "Linux gate pages are retained for reuse until Runtime destruction, one 4 KiB page per peak simultaneous live gate.",
    "No new throughput, concurrent kernel overlap or wait-mode latency gain is established.",
    "Callback release and owner completion waits run in separate threads; actual OS sleep occurrence and scheduling overlap were not instrumented.",
    "Full Strata queue/device/event adaptation, async recording, allocation lifetime and larger Windows logical allocations remain unfinished."
  ],
